{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"teenage-unquieted-ringworm","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Observation\n\nObserved 2026-10-18 in a Claude Code session on the rebar checkout (the main checkout, origin/main at b97d414b8e2), driving the tracker through the configured `rebar` MCP server.\n\nSequence, all within about three minutes:\n\n1. Nine `next_batch` calls issued in one parallel batch (one per open complexity-reduction epic). Every one returned the client error `MCP server \"rebar\" is not connected`. No server-side error text, no partial result.\n2. A single `show_ticket` call issued alone immediately afterwards succeeded.\n3. The same nine `next_batch` calls re-issued as a parallel batch all failed again with the identical error.\n4. A single `next_batch` issued alone (`unhygienic-houndlike-arrowworm`) succeeded.\n5. Earlier in the same session, a batch of five parallel calls (three `show_ticket`, two `next_batch`) had succeeded, and a batch of three (`list_tickets`, `search`, plus a Bash call) had succeeded.\n\nThe local CLI (`.venv/bin/rebar next-batch <epic>`) answered every one of the nine queries correctly, so the store and the resolver are fine; the failure is in the MCP transport or server process.\n\n## What the evidence rules out\n\n- Not a store or resolver fault: the local CLI returned the same batches.\n- Not an auth fault: single calls succeeded before and after each failure.\n- Not a specific tool: `next_batch` succeeded alone and failed only in the batch.\n\n## Hypotheses (unproven)\n\n- The server process crashes or is restarted when N concurrent requests arrive (N somewhere between 5 and 9 in this session), and the client reports the dead connection for the whole batch.\n- The client's per-server concurrency handling marks the server disconnected when one request in a batch fails or times out, and that state poisons the sibling requests.\n- `next_batch` on a large epic is slow enough that nine at once exceed a server-side or client-side deadline, which the client surfaces as \"not connected\" rather than as a timeout.\n\n## Reproduction to try\n\nFrom a Claude Code session with the rebar MCP server configured, issue 9 `next_batch` calls in one parallel batch against the open `complexity-reduction` epics. Then issue them one at a time. Compare. Capture the MCP server's stderr/log for the failing batch (the server-side log location is not documented in `docs/mcp-reference.md` or `docs/mcp-client-setup.md`; finding it is part of this ticket).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- AC1: The root cause of the \"not connected\" state under parallel requests is identified with server-side evidence (log or stack trace), and recorded on this ticket.\n- AC2: Either the server survives at least 9 concurrent read-tool requests, or the client receives a specific, actionable error (a per-request timeout or an explicit concurrency limit) instead of a connection-dropped error that fails the sibling requests.\n- AC3: A test covers the concurrency behaviour at the MCP transport layer (concurrent read requests complete independently; one slow request does not fail its siblings).\n- AC4: `docs/mcp-reference.md` or `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` names where the server writes its log, so the next operator can collect it without reading source.\n\n## File impact (expected)\n\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_server.py` or wherever the transport/request loop lives (to be confirmed during investigation)\n- `tests/unit/mcp/` (new concurrency test)\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md` or `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` (log location)","id":"dff5-6fe2-646c-4a0e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","reliability"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"rebar MCP server drops its connection under parallel tool calls; every call in the batch fails \"MCP server rebar is not connected\""},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1792360361814363001,"uuid":"dcbaa6b8-872b-4460-b3cf-2889f1231e1d"}